  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper text is present. The text labels it as an ICLR 2026 conference paper, despite the supplied venue/year metadata.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study extends scaling analysis to geospatial representations, testing how downstream task performance varies with pretraining data size and spatial coverage rather than testing language-model loss scaling.",
    "gate_note": "The study does not measure language-model cross-entropy, parameter/token/compute scaling, power-law fits, or compute-optimal allocation. Its own relevant results are downstream R² scores for MoRA variants pretrained on three spatial coverages. The LLM scaling-law results cited in the paper are not recorded as rows.",
    "test_reason": "The study does not test any version. Its scaling experiment pretrains a geospatial GNN and contrastive embedding model (MoRA) on three nested spatial coverages: 3,904, 28,855 and 195,574 H3 cells. It reports downstream R² in Jiangsu only. There is no language model, no held-out pretraining loss, no parameter or compute variation and no power-law fit. With three points, the power-law form (V5) cannot be assessed. V6 concerns language-model downstream performance, so the system kind does not match. The authors say the results 'echo' LLM scaling laws, but the result is a related, different construct and is recorded under fits_no_version. If the project reads V6 as covering any pretrained representation model, these rows would weakly support it. They would also limit its scope, since EDU, HSR and HOU are non-monotonic and CRI and NTL gains are within noise.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors say downstream performance 'generally increases' with pretraining data size and spatial coverage. They report large gains from Jiangsu to East China and marginal gains from East China to China. They call this consistent with scaling in language and vision, and describe it as the first evidence of scaling laws in geospatial representation learning. They fit no scaling law.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses three nested spatial-coverage/data-size variants of a geospatial representation model and measures downstream R². It does not use language-model held-out cross-entropy, vary parameter count, tokens, or compute, or fit power laws.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Performance varies with spatial coverage; gains are described as especially large from Jiangsu to East China and relatively marginal from East China to China. EDU, HSR, and HOU are non-monotonic across the three coverages.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "For a mobility-anchored geospatial embedding model, downstream R² in Jiangsu mostly rises with pretraining coverage (3.9k→28.9k→195.6k cells), with diminishing gains. EDU, HSR and HOU are non-monotonic, and CRI and NTL gains are negligible at some steps.",
        "proposed_claim": "Downstream performance of non-language pretrained representation models improves, with diminishing returns, as pretraining data size grows.",
        "row_ids": [
          "T7-EC-POP",
          "T7-CN-POP",
